:[{"DocComment":" Interprets `src` as having type `&Dst`, and then reads `src` without moving"},{"DocComment":" the contained value."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This function will unsafely assume the pointer `src` is valid for [`size_of::<Dst>`][size_of]"},{"DocComment":" bytes by transmuting `&Src` to `&Dst` and then reading the `&Dst` (except that this is done"},{"DocComment":" in a way that is correct even when `&Dst` has stricter alignment requirements than `&Src`)."},{"DocComment":" It will also unsafely create a copy of the contained value instead of moving out of `src`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" It is not a compile-time error if `Src` and `Dst` have different sizes, but it"},{"DocComment":" is highly encouraged to only invoke this function where `Src` and `Dst` have the"},{"DocComment":" same size. This function triggers [undefined behavior][ub] if `Dst` is larger than"},{"DocComment":" `Src`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [ub]: ../../reference/behavior-considered-undefined.html"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::mem;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" #[repr(packed)]"},{"DocComment":" struct Foo {"},{"DocComment":"     bar: u8,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let foo_array = [10u8];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     // Copy the data from 'foo_array' and treat it as a 'Foo'"},{"DocComment":"     let mut foo_struct: Foo = mem::transmute_copy(&foo_array);"},{"DocComment":"     assert_eq!(foo_struct.bar, 10);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":"     // Modify the copied data"},{"DocComment":"     foo_struct.bar = 20;"},{"DocComment":"     assert_eq!(foo_struct.bar, 20);"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // The contents of 'foo_array' should not have changed"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(foo_array, [10]);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"Builtin":{"TrackCaller":{"data":{"file_id":1,"beg":{"line":1070,"col":0},"end":{"line":1070,"col":15}},"generated_from_span":null}}}],"inline":"Hint","rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null,"diagnostic_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[{"index":0,"name":null,"variance":"Covariant","mutability":"Unknown"}],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"signature":{"is_unsafe":true,"abi":"Rust","is_variadic":false,"inputs":[{"HashConsedValue":[588,{"Ref":[{"Var":{"Bound":[0,0]}},{"Deduplicated":312},"Shared"]}]}],"output":{"Deduplicated":231}},"src":"TopLevel","is_global_initializer":null,"body":"Opaque"},{"def_id":3,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["intrinsics",0]},{"Ident":["transmute",0]},{"Instantiated":{"params":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"skip_binder":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":250},{"Deduplicated":244}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]},"kind":"Other"}}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":3,"beg":{"line":843,"col":0},"end":{"line":843,"col":57}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Reinterprets the bits of a value of one type as another type."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Both types must have the same size. Compilation will fail if this is not guaranteed."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `transmute` is semantically equivalent to a bitwise move of one type"},{"DocComment":" into another. It copies the bits from the source value into the"},{"DocComment":" destination value, then forgets the original. Note that source and destination"},{"DocComment":" are passed by-value, which means if `Src` or `Dst` contain padding, that padding"},{"DocComment":" is *not* guaranteed to be preserved by `transmute`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Both the argument and the result must be [valid](../../nomicon/what-unsafe-does.html) at"},{"DocComment":" their given type. Violating this condition leads to [undefined behavior][ub]. The compiler"},{"DocComment":" will generate code *assuming that you, the programmer, ensure that there will never be"},{"DocComment":" undefined behavior*. It is therefore your responsibility to guarantee that every value"},{"DocComment":" passed to `transmute` is valid at both types `Src` and `Dst`. Failing to uphold this condition"},{"DocComment":" may lead to unexpected and unstable compilation results. This makes `transmute` **incredibly"},{"DocComment":" unsafe**. `transmute` should be the absolute last resort."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Because `transmute` is a by-value operation, alignment of the *transmuted values"},{"DocComment":" themselves* is not a concern. As with any other function, the compiler already ensures"},{"DocComment":" both `Src` and `Dst` are properly aligned. However, when transmuting values that *point"},{"DocComment":" elsewhere* (such as pointers, references, boxes…), the caller has to ensure proper"},{"DocComment":" alignment of the pointed-to values."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The [nomicon](../../nomicon/transmutes.html) has additional documentation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [ub]: ../../reference/behavior-considered-undefined.html"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Transmutation between pointers and integers"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Special care has to be taken when transmuting between pointers and integers, e.g."},{"DocComment":" transmuting between `*const ()` and `usize`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Transmuting *pointers to integers* in a `const` context is [undefined behavior][ub], unless"},{"DocComment":" the pointer was originally created *from* an integer. (That includes this function"},{"DocComment":" specifically, integer-to-pointer casts, and helpers like [`dangling`][crate::ptr::dangling],"},{"DocComment":" but also semantically-equivalent conversions such as punning through `repr(C)` union"},{"DocComment":" fields.) Any attempt to use the resulting value for integer operations will abort"},{"DocComment":" const-evaluation. (And even outside `const`, such transmutation is touching on many"},{"DocComment":" unspecified aspects of the Rust memory model and should be avoided. See below for"},{"DocComment":" alternatives.)"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Transmuting *integers to pointers* is a largely unspecified operation. It is likely *not*"},{"DocComment":" equivalent to an `as` cast. Doing non-zero-sized memory accesses with a pointer constructed"},{"DocComment":" this way is currently considered undefined behavior."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" All this also applies when the integer is nested inside an array, tuple, struct, or enum."},{"DocComment":" However, `MaybeUninit<usize>` is not considered an integer type for the purpose of this"},{"DocComment":" section. Transmuting `*const ()` to `MaybeUninit<usize>` is fine---but then calling"},{"DocComment":" `assume_init()` on that result is considered as completing the pointer-to-integer transmute"},{"DocComment":" and thus runs into the issues discussed above."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In particular, doing a pointer-to-integer-to-pointer roundtrip via `transmute` is *not* a"},{"DocComment":" lossless process. If you want to round-trip a pointer through an integer in a way that you"},{"DocComment":" can get back the original pointer, you need to use `as` casts, or replace the integer type"},{"DocComment":" by `MaybeUninit<$int>` (and never call `assume_init()`). If you are looking for a way to"},{"DocComment":" store data of arbitrary type, also use `MaybeUninit<T>` (that will also handle uninitialized"},{"DocComment":" memory due to padding). If you specifically need to store something that is \"either an"},{"DocComment":" integer or a pointer\", use `*mut ()`: integers can be converted to pointers and back without"},{"DocComment":" any loss (via `as` casts or via `transmute`)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" There are a few things that `transmute` is really useful for."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Turning a pointer into a function pointer. This is *not* portable to"},{"DocComment":" machines where function pointers and data pointers have different sizes."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" fn foo() -> i32 {"},{"DocComment":"     0"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" // Crucially, we `as`-cast to a raw pointer before `transmute`ing to a function pointer."},{"DocComment":" // This avoids an integer-to-pointer `transmute`, which can be problematic."},{"DocComment":" // Transmuting between raw pointers and function pointers (i.e., two pointer types) is fine."},{"DocComment":" let pointer = foo as fn() -> i32 as *const ();"},{"DocComment":" let function = unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     std::mem::transmute::<*const (), fn() -> i32>(pointer)"},{"DocComment":" };"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(function(), 0);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Extending a lifetime, or shortening an invariant lifetime. This is"},{"DocComment":" advanced, very unsafe Rust!"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" struct R<'a>(&'a i32);"},{"DocComment":" unsafe fn extend_lifetime<'b>(r: R<'b>) -> R<'static> {"},{"DocComment":"     unsafe { std::mem::transmute::<R<'b>, R<'static>>(r) }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" unsafe fn shorten_invariant_lifetime<'b, 'c>(r: &'b mut R<'static>)"},{"DocComment":"                                              -> &'b mut R<'c> {"},{"DocComment":"     unsafe { std::mem::transmute::<&'b mut R<'static>, &'b mut R<'c>>(r) }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Alternatives"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Don't despair: many uses of `transmute` can be achieved through other means."},{"DocComment":" Below are common applications of `transmute` which can be replaced with safer"},{"DocComment":" constructs."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Turning raw bytes (`[u8; SZ]`) into `u32`, `f64`, etc.:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(unnecessary_transmutes)]"},{"DocComment":" let raw_bytes = [0x78, 0x56, 0x34, 0x12];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let num = unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     std::mem::transmute::<[u8; 4], u32>(raw_bytes)"},{"DocComment":" };"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // use `u32::from_ne_bytes` instead"},{"DocComment":" let num = u32::from_ne_bytes(raw_bytes);"},{"DocComment":" // or use `u32::from_le_bytes` or `u32::from_be_bytes` to specify the endianness"},{"DocComment":" let num = u32::from_le_bytes(raw_bytes);"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(num, 0x12345678);"},{"DocComment":" let num = u32::from_be_bytes(raw_bytes);"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(num, 0x78563412);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Turning a pointer into a `usize`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```no_run"},{"DocComment":" let ptr = &0;"},{"DocComment":" let ptr_num_transmute = unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     std::mem::transmute::<&i32, usize>(ptr)"},{"DocComment":" };"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Use an `as` cast instead"},{"DocComment":" let ptr_num_cast = ptr as *const i32 as usize;"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that using `transmute` to turn a pointer to a `usize` is (as noted above) [undefined"},{"DocComment":" behavior][ub] in `const` contexts. Also outside of consts, this operation might not behave"},{"DocComment":" as expected -- this is touching on many unspecified aspects of the Rust memory model."},{"DocComment":" Depending on what the code is doing, the following alternatives are preferable to"},{"DocComment":" pointer-to-integer transmutation:"},{"DocComment":" - If the code just wants to store data of arbitrary type in some buffer and needs to pick a"},{"DocComment":"   type for that buffer, it can use [`MaybeUninit`][crate::mem::MaybeUninit]."},{"DocComment":" - If the code actually wants to work on the address the pointer points to, it can use `as`"},{"DocComment":"   casts or [`ptr.addr()`][pointer::addr]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Turning a `*mut T` into a `&mut T`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let ptr: *mut i32 = &mut 0;"},{"DocComment":" let ref_transmuted = unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     std::mem::transmute::<*mut i32, &mut i32>(ptr)"},{"DocComment":" };"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Use a reborrow instead"},{"DocComment":" let ref_casted = unsafe { &mut *ptr };"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Turning a `&mut T` into a `&mut U`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let ptr = &mut 0;"},{"DocComment":" let val_transmuted = unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     std::mem::transmute::<&mut i32, &mut u32>(ptr)"},{"DocComment":" };"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Now, put together `as` and reborrowing - note the chaining of `as`"},{"DocComment":" // `as` is not transitive"},{"DocComment":" let val_casts = unsafe { &mut *(ptr as *mut i32 as *mut u32) };"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Turning a `&str` into a `&[u8]`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" // this is not a good way to do this."},{"DocComment":" let slice = unsafe { std::mem::transmute::<&str, &[u8]>(\"Rust\") };"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(slice, &[82, 117, 115, 116]);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // You could use `str::as_bytes`"},{"DocComment":" let slice = \"Rust\".as_bytes();"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(slice, &[82, 117, 115, 116]);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Or, just use a byte string, if you have control over the string"},{"DocComment":" // literal"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(b\"Rust\", &[82, 117, 115, 116]);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Turning a `Vec<&T>` into a `Vec<Option<&T>>`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" To transmute the inner type of the contents of a container, you must make sure to not"},{"DocComment":" violate any of the container's invariants. For `Vec`, this means that both the size"},{"DocComment":" *and alignment* of the inner types have to match. Other containers might rely on the"},{"DocComment":" size of the type, alignment, or even the `TypeId`, in which case transmuting wouldn't"},{"DocComment":" be possible at all without violating the container invariants."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let store = [0, 1, 2, 3];"},{"DocComment":" let v_orig = store.iter().collect::<Vec<&i32>>();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // clone the vector as we will reuse them later"},{"DocComment":" let v_clone = v_orig.clone();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Using transmute: this relies on the unspecified data layout of `Vec`, which is a"},{"DocComment":" // bad idea and could cause Undefined Behavior."},{"DocComment":" // However, it is no-copy."},{"DocComment":" let v_transmuted = unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     std::mem::transmute::<Vec<&i32>, Vec<Option<&i32>>>(v_clone)"},{"DocComment":" };"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let v_clone = v_orig.clone();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // This is the suggested, safe way."},{"DocComment":" // It may copy the entire vector into a new one though, but also may not."},{"DocComment":" let v_collected = v_clone.into_iter()"},{"DocComment":"                          .map(Some)"},{"DocComment":"                          .collect::<Vec<Option<&i32>>>();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let v_clone = v_orig.clone();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // This is the proper no-copy, unsafe way of \"transmuting\" a `Vec`, without relying on the"},{"DocComment":" // data layout. Instead of literally calling `transmute`, we perform a pointer cast, but"},{"DocComment":" // in terms of converting the original inner type (`&i32`) to the new one (`Option<&i32>`),"},{"DocComment":" // this has all the same caveats. Besides the information provided above, also consult the"},{"DocComment":" // [`from_raw_parts`] documentation."},{"DocComment":" let (ptr, len, capacity) = v_clone.into_raw_parts();"},{"DocComment":" let v_from_raw = unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     Vec::from_raw_parts(ptr.cast::<*mut Option<&i32>>(), len, capacity)"},{"DocComment":" };"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`from_raw_parts`]: ../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.from_raw_parts"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Implementing `split_at_mut`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::{slice, mem};"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // There are multiple ways to do this, and there are multiple problems"},{"DocComment":" // with the following (transmute) way."},{"DocComment":" fn split_at_mut_transmute<T>(slice: &mut [T], mid: usize)"},{"DocComment":"                              -> (&mut [T], &mut [T]) {"},{"DocComment":"     let len = slice.len();"},{"DocComment":"     assert!(mid <= len);"},{"DocComment":"     unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"         let slice2 = mem::transmute::<&mut [T], &mut [T]>(slice);"},{"DocComment":"         // first: transmute is not type safe; all it checks is that T and"},{"DocComment":"         // U are of the same size. Second, right here, you have two"},{"DocComment":"         // mutable references pointing to the same memory."},{"DocComment":"         (&mut slice[0..mid], &mut slice2[mid..len])"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // This gets rid of the type safety problems; `&mut *` will *only* give"},{"DocComment":" // you a `&mut T` from a `&mut T` or `*mut T`."},{"DocComment":" fn split_at_mut_casts<T>(slice: &mut [T], mid: usize)"},{"DocComment":"                          -> (&mut [T], &mut [T]) {"},{"DocComment":"     let len = slice.len();"},{"DocComment":"     assert!(mid <= len);"},{"DocComment":"     unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"         let slice2 = &mut *(slice as *mut [T]);"},{"DocComment":"         // however, you still have two mutable references pointing to"},{"DocComment":"         // the same memory."},{"DocComment":"         (&mut slice[0..mid], &mut slice2[mid..len])"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // This is how the standard library does it. This is the best method, if"},{"DocComment":" // you need to do something like this"},{"DocComment":" fn split_at_stdlib<T>(slice: &mut [T], mid: usize)"},{"DocComment":"                       -> (&mut [T], &mut [T]) {"},{"DocComment":"     let len = slice.len();"},{"DocComment":"     assert!(mid <= len);"},{"DocComment":"     unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"         let ptr = slice.as_mut_ptr();"},{"DocComment":"         // This now has three mutable references pointing at the same"},{"DocComment":"         // memory. `slice`, the rvalue ret.0, and the rvalue ret.1."},{"DocComment":"         // `slice` is never used after `let ptr = ...`, and so one can"},{"DocComment":"         // treat it as \"dead\", and therefore, you only have two real"},{"DocComment":"         // mutable slices."},{"DocComment":"         (slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr, mid),"},{"DocComment":"          slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr.add(mid), len - mid))"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"Builtin":{"RustcDiagnosticItem":"transmute"}},{"Builtin":"RustcIntrinsic"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null,"diagnostic_item":"transmute"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"signature":{"is_unsafe":true,"abi":"Rust","is_variadic":false,"inputs":[{"Deduplicated":250}],"output":{"Deduplicated":244}},"src":"TopLevel","is_global_initializer":null,"body":{"Intrinsic":{"name":"transmute","arg_names":["src"]}}}],"global_decls":[],"trait_decls":[],"